  "requirement": "The `habeas-refactor` skill MUST tell agents not to edit evidence files by hand\nto follow a move, and that renaming a function or editing moved text is not a\nmove and needs reassessment.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement obliges the `habeas-refactor` skill to tell agents three things: (1) do not edit evidence files by hand to follow a move; (2) renaming a function is not a move and needs reassessment; (3) editing moved text is not a move and needs reassessment.\n\nThe single test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_never_hand_edits_evidence_and_a_rename_is_not_a_move]`, carries a `{test}` reference. It reads the real bundled `habeas-refactor` `SKILL.md` and extracts the `## Gotchas` section with `section`. It then calls `assert_text`, which panics if any phrase is missing. The phrases map to the obligations as follows:\n- Obligation 1: \"Never edit evidence files by hand to follow a move\".\n- Obligation 2: \"A rename is not a move. Renaming a function, type, or other item changes its code\" and \"its evidence needs reassessment\".\n- Obligation 3: \"Editing moved code or text in the same step leaves every record that read it stale\".\n\nThe assertions are real, because `assert_text` uses `contains` and fails on any absent phrase. They match the skill's actual gotcha paragraphs in `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md`. Dropping any of the three messages from the skill would make the test fail.\n\nThe skill states obligation 3 as \"stale\" records. The test asserts that wording, so it follows the skill's own text. I treat this as an adequate proxy for \"needs reassessment\", not as a gap.\n\nNo `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply, and I found no marking gaps or missing scenarios.",
